<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahnameh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1010</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Shahnama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shahnameh, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Book of Kings</a>, is the national epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, completed by the poet Ferdowsi in about 1010 after some thirty years of work. In about 50,000 couplets of Persian verse it tells the history of Iran from the mythical first king to the Arab conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, with heroes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>. It preserved Persian legend and language and was widely copied in illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahnameh.jpg" alt="Shahnameh" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Sultan Muhammad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Court_of_Gayumars_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
